---
import Page from '../../layouts/Page.astro';
import { sitePath } from '../../lib/urls';
---

<Page
  article
  title="Followers vs. following: understand the overlap"
  description="A concrete example explains followers, following, mutuals, and the two non-mutual groups, with clear limits for incomplete source data."
>
  <p class="eyebrow">GUIDE · THE BASICS</p><h1>Followers vs. following.</h1><p
    class="lede"
  >
    The words look similar, but the direction changes everything. Your followers
    list points toward you. Your following list points toward other accounts.
  </p>
  <h2>Start with a small example</h2><p>
    Imagine your supplied followers list contains <strong
      >ada, ben, and cam</strong
    >. Your supplied following list contains <strong>ben, cam, and dev</strong>.
    These names are fictional.
  </p>
  <div class="comparison-table">
    <table>
      <caption>What the two fictional lists show</caption><thead
        ><tr
          ><th scope="col">Group</th><th scope="col">Accounts</th><th
            scope="col">Why</th
          ></tr
        ></thead
      ><tbody
        ><tr
          ><th scope="row">Mutuals</th><td>ben, cam</td><td
            >Present in both lists.</td
          ></tr
        ><tr
          ><th scope="row">Not following you back</th><td>dev</td><td
            >In following, absent from followers.</td
          ></tr
        ><tr
          ><th scope="row">You don’t follow back</th><td>ada</td><td
            >In followers, absent from following.</td
          ></tr
        ></tbody
      >
    </table>
  </div>
  <p>
    The uploaded lists are the working dataset. If a known follower part is
    missing, dev is labeled “Not found in supplied followers,” with the
    limitation shown. The comparison remains useful without certifying current
    Instagram relationships.
  </p>
  <h2>Counts do not tell you who overlaps</h2><p>
    Two accounts can both have 6,000 followers and follow 6,000 accounts, yet
    have very different mutual groups. Matching totals do not identify
    individual accounts. Complete account lists are necessary to compare
    relationships, and a count match alone cannot establish that a source was
    not sampled or truncated.
  </p>
  <h2>Follow-back is a description, not an obligation</h2><p>
    A one-way connection can be intentional: someone may follow a publication,
    artist, or community without expecting a return follow. The report describes
    supplied data. It does not score friendships, assign motives, or tell you
    whom to unfollow.
  </p>
  <h2>Read the source before the result</h2><p>
    Your optional source details explain what the files represent. Basic upload
    comparison does not require an account label or date. A recent import can
    contain an older export. Separate collections may also have different time
    windows; neither is a perfect atomic record of a changing platform.
  </p>
  <p>
    Next: <a href={sitePath('/guides/not-following-back-vs-unfollowed/')}
      >why “not following back” and “unfollowed” mean different things</a
    >, or <a href={sitePath('/')}>see the current checker preview</a>.
  </p>
  <p><a href={sitePath('/guides/')}>Browse all MutualLens guides</a>.</p>
</Page>
